<!-- BEGIN baseline:code-review-reporting v0.1.0 -->
## Portable Agent Baseline: Code Review Reporting

- Diff a candidate finding against sibling implementations before reporting it — a deviation-shaped observation is only a finding if it actually deviates. In a monorepo, plugin tree, or set of services from one template, one `grep -rn "<pattern>" --include=<ext> <siblings>/` decides it: only this component does it → real finding; every sibling does it → convention, and if it's genuinely bad that's a separate repo-wide item, not this PR's problem; siblings split → compare against the maintained reference ones the repo's own rules file names. State the comparison in the review ("matches all 14 front-ends") so the next reviewer doesn't re-raise it, and list the checked non-findings briefly. Convention is not correctness — the check tells you whose problem it is, not whether it's fine.
- A review thread marked resolved is the author's claim, not evidence: on a PR already reviewed by a bot or a person, read the current source at each thread's location before scoping those topics out. It prevents duplicate feedback, stops a topic being silently dropped by both passes, and the "already fixed" lines are where the first pass's missed cause usually still sits — a reviewer that fixes a symptom often leaves it on the same line. Say "verified all N prior threads are addressed in current code; below is what's left". Verification, not re-litigation.
- When a fix hinges on an unmade product decision, enumerate the defensible answers and test current behaviour against each before reporting it as blocked. If it satisfies **none**, it is a confirmed defect — report it as one with the fix per branch, leading with the table, not as a question handed back. Check whether sibling implementations already answer it by convention; they often do, which removes the blocker outright. Only if current behaviour satisfies one candidate is it genuinely a decision, and then name which one it implements so the choice is "keep or change". Still surface a genuinely user-visible divergence — alongside the confirmed defect, not instead of it.
- In a CI that dispatches per-package by script name and skips any package that doesn't define it, a new package's `scripts` block is its coverage declaration — read it against the pipeline's stage list before reading the diff. Every stage it omits is a stage that silently never runs, printing one indented line into a log nobody reads while the build stays green. Report it as coverage ("defines typecheck and build; CI skips lint and test for this package entirely") and point at the nearest sibling that defines the missing stage, so adding it is a copy. Generalises to any convention-over-configuration CI: absence is configuration, and absence is invisible in a green build. Not every package needs every stage — the finding is that nobody decided.

Apply this baseline whenever reviewing someone else's change, but never use it to override explicit user instructions, safety rules, privacy boundaries, or stricter repo-local instructions. It does not cover critiquing a design proposal (`design-review`) or authoring a PR description (`pr-description`).
<!-- END baseline:code-review-reporting -->
